======================================================================
APPROACH 2: THE INDIVIDUAL TRIPLE BOUNDARY PROJECTION
======================================================================

Key idea: for any transitive triple σ in T, the vector ∂₂(σ) is a row
of C_full. Its projection onto im(P) is P·∂₂(σ).

C_T^TC_T · (P·∂₂(σ)) = C_T^T·(C_T·P·∂₂(σ))

If P·∂₂(σ) happens to be in ker(C_R), then C_T^TC_T·(P·∂₂(σ)) = n·P·∂₂(σ).

When is P·∂₂(σ) ∈ ker(C_R)?
  C_R·P·∂₂(σ) = 0
  ⟺ ∂₂(τ)·P·∂₂(σ) = 0 for every cyclic triple τ
  ⟺ (C_full·C_full^T)_{τ,σ}/n · n = 0  ... hmm
  Actually P = (1/n) C_full^T C_full, so
  C_R · P · ∂₂(σ)^T = (1/n) C_R C_full^T C_full ∂₂(σ)^T
  = (1/n) C_R (n P) ∂₂(σ)^T? No, C_full^T C_full = n P acts on ℝ^E.
  ∂₂(σ) ∈ ℝ^E. So n P ∂₂(σ) = n · P ∂₂(σ).

Wait: P is projection onto im(C_full^T). P∂₂(σ) = P·(row of C_full^T) ...
Actually ∂₂(σ) is a ROW of C_full, or equivalently a COLUMN of C_full^T.
So ∂₂(σ) ∈ im(C_full^T) if and only if ∂₂(σ) is in the column space of C_full^T.
But C_full^T has columns = rows of C_full = ∂₂ of all triples.
So ∂₂(σ) IS a column of C_full^T, hence ∂₂(σ) ∈ im(C_full^T).
Therefore P·∂₂(σ) = ∂₂(σ).

So: C_R·∂₂(σ) = [<∂₂(τ), ∂₂(σ)> for each cyclic τ].
This is the column of C_full·C_full^T corresponding to σ, restricted to cyclic rows.

C_full·C_full^T has ALL eigenvalues = n (on rank space) or 0.
So (C_full·C_full^T)·e_σ = n·P'·e_σ where P' is the projection in triple-space.

If P'·e_σ has zero components on all cyclic triples, then C_R·∂₂(σ)=0.
This happens iff e_σ (unit vector in triple space) has its P'-image
entirely supported on transitive triples.

But P' is NOT the identity — it's the projection onto the column space
of C_full (in triple-space). The nullspace of C_full·C_full^T is the
nullspace of C_full^T (dependency space of triples).


